# Router Configurations

CLI configuration excerpts as entered in Packet Tracer for the MOH and WHC routers, covering interface addressing, RIPv2, and static route redistribution. (Extend this file with CSC/DDU/DPU/RPP configs directly from your `.pkt` file if you want the full set documented — Packet Tracer's `show running-config` on each device will give you the exact text.)

## MOH Router

```
Router(config)#hostname MOH
MOH(config)#interface g0/0
MOH(config-if)#ip address 41.41.0.1 255.255.255.0
MOH(config-if)#no shutdown
MOH(config-if)#exit

MOH(config)#interface s0/3/0
MOH(config-if)#ip address 41.41.5.1 255.255.255.252
MOH(config-if)#no shutdown
MOH(config-if)#exit

MOH(config)#interface g0/1
MOH(config-if)#ip address 41.41.5.17 255.255.255.252
MOH(config-if)#no shutdown
MOH(config-if)#exit

MOH(config)#interface s0/3/0
MOH(config-if)#clock rate 64000
MOH(config-if)#exit

MOH(config)#router rip
MOH(config-router)#version 2
MOH(config-router)#no auto-summary
MOH(config-router)#network 41.0.0.0
MOH(config-router)#exit
```

### Static route + redistribution (MOH)

A static route was required to reach the DDU network, then redistributed into RIP so the rest of the RIP domain learns the path:

```
MOH(config)#ip route 41.41.3.0 255.255.255.0 41.41.5.2
MOH(config)#router rip
MOH(config-router)#redistribute static
MOH(config-router)#exit
MOH(config)#end
MOH#write memory
```

## WHC Router

```
Router(config)#hostname WHC
WHC(config)#interface g0/0
WHC(config-if)#ip address 41.41.1.1 255.255.255.0
WHC(config-if)#no shutdown
WHC(config-if)#interface s0/3/0
WHC(config-if)#ip address 41.41.5.2 255.255.255.252
WHC(config-if)#no shutdown
WHC(config-if)#exit

WHC(config)#interface s0/3/1
WHC(config-if)#exit

WHC(config)#router rip
WHC(config-router)#version 2
WHC(config-router)#no auto-summary
WHC(config-router)#network 41.0.0.0
WHC(config-router)#exit
```

## Notes on the routing design

- **RIPv2** was chosen over RIPv1 specifically because it supports **classless routing** (carries subnet mask info in updates), which is required here since the network uses **VLSM** — RIPv1 would break on the mixed `/24`, `/25`, `/26`, and `/30` masks in this design.
- **`no auto-summary`** is critical: without it, RIP would summarize `41.41.x.0/24` routes back to the classful `41.0.0.0/8` boundary at area borders, which would break routing between the differently-sized VLSM subnets.
- The **static route on MOH** (`41.41.3.0/24 via 41.41.5.2`) plus `redistribute static` shows a common real-world pattern: using a static route to patch a path RIP couldn't converge on cleanly, then injecting it back into the dynamic routing domain so other routers still learn it automatically.
- The **WHC S0/2/0 → CSC (backup)** and **CSC S0/3/1 → WHC (backup)** interfaces on `41.41.5.20/30`–`41.41.5.22` form the redundant path — if the primary MOH↔CSC backbone segment fails, RIP recalculates and traffic can still reach CSC via WHC.

